1
00:00:06,047 --> 00:00:07,465
[upbeat music plays]

2
00:00:09,509 --> 00:00:10,510
[engine turns over]

3
00:00:11,136 --> 00:00:12,387
[engine revs, horn honks]

4
00:00:13,555 --> 00:00:17,475
[Jerry] This is a 1966
Porsche 356SC Cabriolet

5
00:00:17,559 --> 00:00:18,852
in light ivory.

6
00:00:18,935 --> 00:00:21,187
It weighs 1,980 pounds.

7
00:00:21,271 --> 00:00:24,649
It has a powerful
1.6 liter flat-four engine.

8
00:00:25,108 --> 00:00:27,652
This car was at one time
a Dutch police car.

9
00:00:29,904 --> 00:00:35,035
In 1966, Porsche had finished building
the 356 and began producing the 911.

10
00:00:35,368 --> 00:00:38,621
But the Dutch Police Department
like convertibles for their visibility,

11
00:00:38,788 --> 00:00:41,082
to catch those Dutch crooks
that are always up to something.

12
00:00:41,166 --> 00:00:42,959
Who made the hole in the dike
to begin with?

13
00:00:43,043 --> 00:00:44,544
And who cut this kid's hair like that?

14
00:00:44,627 --> 00:00:46,838
I'd like to ask them a few questions.
Let's round 'em up.

15
00:00:46,921 --> 00:00:48,131
[siren wails]

16
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:50,050
So they asked Porsche,

17
00:00:50,133 --> 00:00:53,595
"Can you just make us ten more
 of the car that you used to make

18
00:00:53,678 --> 00:00:55,263
and don't make any more?"

19
00:00:55,513 --> 00:00:57,849
Imagine asking a car company today,

20
00:00:57,932 --> 00:01:00,268
"Hey, remember that car you made
a couple years ago?

21
00:01:00,351 --> 00:01:01,644
We need ten more of 'em."

22
00:01:01,728 --> 00:01:03,980
And Porsche went, "Okay, ten more."

23
00:01:04,397 --> 00:01:06,441
So, they retooled
their entire production line

24
00:01:06,524 --> 00:01:10,278
just to make ten more of these
for the Dutch Police Department.

25
00:01:10,361 --> 00:01:11,279
Here they are.

26
00:01:11,488 --> 00:01:14,449
And of the ten they made,
this is the tenth of the ten.

27
00:01:14,783 --> 00:01:18,411
So, it is the very last Porsche 356
ever made.

28
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:20,538
That's why they put the flowers
on the hood.

29
00:01:20,622 --> 00:01:21,873
Here it is in '66.

30
00:01:21,956 --> 00:01:23,625
Here I am driving it today.

31
00:01:23,875 --> 00:01:25,251
[engine turns over]

32
00:01:26,086 --> 00:01:27,754
So, it was the last 356,

33
00:01:27,837 --> 00:01:30,298
but it is also the first car I ever used

34
00:01:30,381 --> 00:01:33,802
for the very first episode
of Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee

35
00:01:33,885 --> 00:01:35,428
with my friend, comedian, writer,

36
00:01:35,512 --> 00:01:38,515
and creator of the Ted L. Nancy
book series, Barry Marder.

37
00:01:39,516 --> 00:01:40,600
So, I had this idea.

38
00:01:40,683 --> 00:01:43,103
But I didn't have the confidence in it 
that you had.

39
00:01:43,186 --> 00:01:46,731
And Barry encouraged me
to make the first show,

40
00:01:46,815 --> 00:01:48,983
which we did back in 2012.

41
00:01:49,067 --> 00:01:51,236
-[Barry makes trumpeting sound]
-[Jerry laughs]

42
00:01:51,319 --> 00:01:54,280
-[Jerry] So, here we are six years later.
-This is what started it.

43
00:01:54,364 --> 00:01:55,782
And I wanted to bring Barry back,

44
00:01:56,116 --> 00:01:58,535
since he was the one
who is really responsible

45
00:01:58,618 --> 00:02:00,453
for me getting this thing going.

46
00:02:00,537 --> 00:02:02,831
Let's toast to what we accomplished.

47
00:02:03,498 --> 00:02:05,542
Barry is one of my closest friends.

48
00:02:05,625 --> 00:02:06,960
We talk everyday.

49
00:02:07,043 --> 00:02:08,419
We always have fun,

50
00:02:08,503 --> 00:02:11,631
and I am looking forward to doing
that same thing again with him today.

51
00:02:11,714 --> 00:02:12,841
[phone rings]

52
00:02:13,216 --> 00:02:14,134
[Barry] Hello?

53
00:02:14,217 --> 00:02:15,135
Hello?

54
00:02:15,218 --> 00:02:16,678
-[Jerry] Hello?
-[Barry] Hello?

55
00:02:17,053 --> 00:02:18,304
-[Jerry] Hello?
-[Barry] Hello?

56
00:02:18,721 --> 00:02:20,765
-[Jerry] Who is this?
-[Barry] Who is this?

57
00:02:20,849 --> 00:02:21,891
[Jerry] What's up, dog?

58
00:02:21,975 --> 00:02:25,770
People think because something happened
in their day where something went awry,

59
00:02:25,854 --> 00:02:27,147
that it's an interesting story.

60
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:28,940
Guess what happened to me?

61
00:02:29,023 --> 00:02:31,901
I went to the dentist.
I had a two o'clock appointment.

62
00:02:32,152 --> 00:02:34,154
Turns out, he couldn't take me till 2:30.

63
00:02:34,237 --> 00:02:36,114
So I sat there for a half an hour.

64
00:02:36,197 --> 00:02:37,991
-[Barry] Yeah.
-[Jerry] It's a bad story.

65
00:02:38,074 --> 00:02:40,243
-[Barry] It's not a good story.
-[Jerry] Shut up.

66
00:02:40,618 --> 00:02:44,038
Well, I do have an exciting car for you.
I'm really looking forward to seeing you.

67
00:02:44,122 --> 00:02:45,874
-Have you had coffee yet?
-[Barry] Nope.

68
00:02:45,957 --> 00:02:48,418
-Great. Be there in a couple minutes.
-[Barry] All right.

69
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:53,047
[Jerry] And that is why
I am Jerry Seinfeld,

70
00:02:53,131 --> 00:02:56,259
and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

71
00:03:00,513 --> 00:03:01,681
[Barry] I love this car.

72
00:03:01,764 --> 00:03:03,141
[engine turns over]

73
00:03:03,224 --> 00:03:04,642
[upbeat music plays]

74
00:03:08,813 --> 00:03:11,232
-[Jerry] What was your first car?
-A used Corvair.

75
00:03:11,524 --> 00:03:13,067
My dad had bought me that car.

76
00:03:13,151 --> 00:03:14,110
The death car.

77
00:03:14,194 --> 00:03:16,863
We were pulling up next to a, uh...
a bookstore.

78
00:03:16,946 --> 00:03:20,700
The whole bookstore was lined
with Unsafe at Any Speed by Ralph Nader.

79
00:03:20,783 --> 00:03:22,535
I said, "Isn't that the car
you bought me?"

80
00:03:23,119 --> 00:03:26,164
Couldn't wait till I turned 16
to buy me a death trap.

81
00:03:26,831 --> 00:03:29,417
You got to admit,
this is a very pleasant experience.

82
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:30,877
[jazz plays]

83
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:33,796
I was looking at this magazine cover.

84
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:36,674
It said, "Caitlyn Jenner Without Makeup."

85
00:03:36,841 --> 00:03:38,134
Isn't that Bruce Jenner?

86
00:03:38,885 --> 00:03:39,802
It is.

87
00:03:41,638 --> 00:03:42,847
[Barry] Where are we headed to?

88
00:03:42,931 --> 00:03:44,766
-Or you're gonna surprise me?
-It's a surprise.

89
00:03:44,849 --> 00:03:49,395
The owner of the parking facility
in front of the deli,

90
00:03:50,438 --> 00:03:53,149
who received his law degree in 1951,

91
00:03:53,733 --> 00:03:55,860
has sent a cease-and-desist letter.

92
00:03:55,944 --> 00:03:57,445
He's threatening legal action.

93
00:03:58,905 --> 00:04:03,785
If we go into the parking lot
and do not shop...

94
00:04:03,868 --> 00:04:05,995
But if you're eating in the deli,
isn't that shopping?

95
00:04:06,079 --> 00:04:08,206
I think the key number here is

96
00:04:08,289 --> 00:04:10,541
-"law degree in '51."
-[Barry laughs]

97
00:04:13,294 --> 00:04:16,381
That is a 7-Eleven
that has been turned into a school.

98
00:04:16,464 --> 00:04:18,091
That looks like the school
that you went to

99
00:04:18,174 --> 00:04:20,260
where you got
your high school equivalency.

100
00:04:20,343 --> 00:04:21,928
I went to, literally, a motel,

101
00:04:22,011 --> 00:04:24,430
'cause I needed this thing
to get into the Air Force.

102
00:04:24,514 --> 00:04:25,723
-Right.
-I told him,

103
00:04:25,807 --> 00:04:27,725
"I... I've never seen the diploma."

104
00:04:28,226 --> 00:04:30,561
And he... he ran it in front of me
really quick.

105
00:04:30,645 --> 00:04:34,274
It was an incidental bill, I think.
My diploma had a club sandwich on it

106
00:04:34,357 --> 00:04:35,400
and some phone charges.

107
00:04:35,483 --> 00:04:36,901
[jazz plays]

108
00:04:38,611 --> 00:04:42,156
I like the fire map on the back door
of the, uh, hotel room.

109
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:44,409
-Yes.
-And it always says, "Don't panic."

110
00:04:44,492 --> 00:04:46,744
-And even if you did panic...
-Yeah.

111
00:04:46,828 --> 00:04:48,454
...you have a perfect excuse.

112
00:04:48,538 --> 00:04:51,082
Gee, I heard they found you naked,
swinging from the shower curtain

113
00:04:51,165 --> 00:04:52,458
with the ice bucket on your head.

114
00:04:52,542 --> 00:04:53,376
-What happened?
-Yeah.

115
00:04:53,459 --> 00:04:54,669
-I panicked.
-I panicked.

116
00:04:55,670 --> 00:04:58,756
This is you and I all day, every day.
-All day, every day.

117
00:05:01,217 --> 00:05:03,428
So, I asked my kids to rate

118
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:08,725
eating, drinking, peeing, and pooping,
one to four.

119
00:05:08,808 --> 00:05:10,310
Eating, drinking, peeing, and pooping.

120
00:05:11,394 --> 00:05:12,895
If you gotta poop, you gotta poop.

121
00:05:12,979 --> 00:05:14,564
So, you're putting that at number one?

122
00:05:14,647 --> 00:05:17,191
-I would say number one.
-I... Oh, that's ridiculous.

123
00:05:17,275 --> 00:05:21,696
Because there's so many more
enjoyable aspects to eating.

124
00:05:21,779 --> 00:05:24,240
You get to think about it, talk about it,

125
00:05:24,532 --> 00:05:26,075
choose where you're gonna eat.

126
00:05:26,159 --> 00:05:29,162
Then, you get to order the food,
you get to anticipate it.

127
00:05:29,245 --> 00:05:31,164
You have... you could have an appetizer.

128
00:05:31,247 --> 00:05:33,833
-You could have a salad.
-You get many second dates?

129
00:05:33,916 --> 00:05:35,168
-[Jerry laughs]
-Huh?

130
00:05:37,628 --> 00:05:39,922
-[Jerry] What a beautiful day.
-[Barry] It is nice out here.

131
00:05:40,006 --> 00:05:41,341
[jazz plays]

132
00:05:49,265 --> 00:05:51,642
I really just want the waitress
to bring me some coffee.

133
00:05:51,726 --> 00:05:53,895
-I have not had any coffee.
-I haven't had any either.

134
00:05:53,978 --> 00:05:55,271
We should get some coffee.

135
00:05:58,399 --> 00:06:00,234
This is what I like about this restaurant.

136
00:06:00,318 --> 00:06:01,694
-Yeah.
-They advertise,

137
00:06:02,153 --> 00:06:05,531
"Guaranteed view of a heavy person
from anywhere in the restaurant."

138
00:06:05,615 --> 00:06:06,949
[Jerry laughing]

139
00:06:07,033 --> 00:06:10,453
The whole place looks like
the Disney Jamboree Bears. Really.

140
00:06:10,536 --> 00:06:13,664
Do you remember when the Disney
Jamboree Bear was an absurd concept?

141
00:06:13,748 --> 00:06:15,708
-The Jamboree Bears at Disney...
-Yeah.

142
00:06:15,792 --> 00:06:17,794
-It was crazy. "Let's take bears...
-Right.

143
00:06:17,877 --> 00:06:19,295
...and put them in human clothes."

144
00:06:19,378 --> 00:06:20,797
Vests, hats,

145
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,423
collar with a bow tie.

146
00:06:22,507 --> 00:06:23,925
Now, it's normal.

147
00:06:24,008 --> 00:06:25,635
And now it's a guy
walking down the street.

148
00:06:25,718 --> 00:06:26,677
[jazz plays]

149
00:06:28,429 --> 00:06:31,224
This is why I love my girlfriend,
even though we're both heavy.

150
00:06:31,849 --> 00:06:34,185
Let's say she's on top of me
and were intimate.

151
00:06:35,436 --> 00:06:37,730
And I'm uncomfortable.
She lets me tap out.

152
00:06:38,439 --> 00:06:40,441
I can go like this
and she'll roll right off.

153
00:06:42,235 --> 00:06:44,946
We are under MMA rules now
during our... our intimacy.

154
00:06:47,281 --> 00:06:50,493
Wow, that coffee is good and strong.
It's hitting me. I love it.

155
00:06:50,576 --> 00:06:51,536
[jazz plays]

156
00:06:52,662 --> 00:06:55,123
This Jeff Sessions looks like
the last surviving Munchkin

157
00:06:55,206 --> 00:06:56,791
from Lollipop Land, doesn't he?

158
00:06:57,333 --> 00:06:58,167
No. He's Elven.

159
00:06:58,251 --> 00:07:00,419
-He's an Elven guy, isn't he?
-Yeah, he's Elven.

160
00:07:01,421 --> 00:07:03,965
You can't get the elf out of a man.

161
00:07:04,507 --> 00:07:05,633
[Jerry laughs]

162
00:07:06,884 --> 00:07:08,678
You can't get the elf out of a man.

163
00:07:08,761 --> 00:07:09,846
[jazz plays]

164
00:07:10,847 --> 00:07:13,516
If I could find
some camouflage pants to fit me,

165
00:07:13,975 --> 00:07:15,476
I would shoot this town up.

166
00:07:19,605 --> 00:07:21,607
I love the little, uh... [clears throat]

167
00:07:22,942 --> 00:07:24,235
People always doing these things.

168
00:07:24,318 --> 00:07:26,154
Yeah. After they eat,
there's always a lot of...

169
00:07:26,237 --> 00:07:27,071
[Barry] Yeah.

170
00:07:27,738 --> 00:07:29,699
Now, I won't eat again till I get home.

171
00:07:30,658 --> 00:07:31,826
[Jerry laughs]

172
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:35,121
-'Cause you're on that fasting program.
-Yep.

173
00:07:38,082 --> 00:07:40,626
-You don't eat anything in between eating.
-Nothing.

174
00:07:40,710 --> 00:07:42,462
[jazz plays]

175
00:07:43,087 --> 00:07:44,213
[woman] How you doing here?

176
00:07:44,297 --> 00:07:45,131
-Fantastic.
-Great.

177
00:07:45,214 --> 00:07:47,842
-Would you like some more?
-Um, I'll wait. Thank you.

178
00:07:48,718 --> 00:07:51,679
Who tells someone, "You want dessert?"
while they're eating?

179
00:07:51,929 --> 00:07:54,182
"Would you like an eclair?" What?

180
00:07:58,019 --> 00:07:59,562
People just drive me crazy.

181
00:08:00,188 --> 00:08:03,691
I was at an event the other day.
I was having a conversation with somebody,

182
00:08:03,774 --> 00:08:06,027
one of the event people
comes over and says,

183
00:08:06,527 --> 00:08:09,572
"We're not doing anything yet,
but I'll let you know."

184
00:08:11,782 --> 00:08:13,034
Then don't say anything.

185
00:08:14,035 --> 00:08:16,287
-Yeah.
-You see I'm having a conversation.

186
00:08:16,370 --> 00:08:18,456
Can't you see people talking?

187
00:08:18,748 --> 00:08:20,249
-Can't you see that?
-No.

188
00:08:21,167 --> 00:08:22,168
[jazz plays]

189
00:08:23,002 --> 00:08:24,503
I just like to talk.

190
00:08:24,587 --> 00:08:25,713
When I say something crazy

191
00:08:25,796 --> 00:08:28,132
and go, "Here's what the psychiatrist
just wrote on his pad."

192
00:08:28,216 --> 00:08:30,301
And he's doodling on his pad
'cause he's got...

193
00:08:30,384 --> 00:08:32,220
he's got seven circles in a row...

194
00:08:32,303 --> 00:08:34,138
-Yeah.
-...and the pyramid with the eye on it.

195
00:08:34,222 --> 00:08:36,432
-Right. Right.
-He's got his own problems. 

196
00:08:36,766 --> 00:08:40,394
You're just the 45-minute, $690 fee.

197
00:08:41,812 --> 00:08:45,066
He goes, "Says he just likes to talk."

198
00:08:45,149 --> 00:08:46,108
[Jerry laughs]

199
00:08:46,192 --> 00:08:47,068
You know?

200
00:08:47,485 --> 00:08:49,487
-While he looks at his watch.
-"Increase promazine."

201
00:08:49,570 --> 00:08:50,404
Yeah.

202
00:08:50,488 --> 00:08:51,364
[jazz plays]

203
00:08:52,823 --> 00:08:54,450
Why do men wear toupees?

204
00:08:54,784 --> 00:08:57,328
-If you are in a bar...
-Yeah.

205
00:08:57,411 --> 00:08:59,830
...and you are talking
to a very attractive young lady... 

206
00:09:00,289 --> 00:09:04,460
But at some point in the relationship
you have to reveal to the person...

207
00:09:06,128 --> 00:09:07,088
"By the way..."

208
00:09:07,171 --> 00:09:09,340
She may have to reveal something to you.

209
00:09:09,799 --> 00:09:11,217
When I was married to Laura,

210
00:09:12,343 --> 00:09:15,346
my fourth wife,
I pulled out a clump of her hair.

211
00:09:16,681 --> 00:09:19,141
-And I thought, "What is this?"
-Yes.

212
00:09:19,517 --> 00:09:21,352
-A clump of hair came out in your hand?
-Right.

213
00:09:21,435 --> 00:09:24,146
It would have had to been
an intimate moment since I never,

214
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:26,357
you know, would ever harm her.

215
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:28,442
She had a weave,

216
00:09:28,526 --> 00:09:30,319
-kind of weave in there.
-[Jerry] Oh.

217
00:09:30,403 --> 00:09:32,071
-[Barry] A big clump.
-Oh, my.

218
00:09:33,322 --> 00:09:35,950
I immediately took a knee 
and blew the whistle.

219
00:09:37,577 --> 00:09:39,870
-And I threw a yellow flag onto the play.
-[Jerry] Yeah.

220
00:09:39,954 --> 00:09:41,289
[upbeat music plays]

221
00:09:46,377 --> 00:09:48,212
We passed Lowe's. [laughs]

222
00:09:48,629 --> 00:09:49,463
[coughs]

223
00:09:49,547 --> 00:09:50,464
You sound great.

224
00:09:50,548 --> 00:09:51,716
[both laugh]

225
00:09:53,342 --> 00:09:55,303
You know, there is such a thing
as overtraining.

226
00:09:58,556 --> 00:10:01,183
-You don't mind if I have a Ricola?
-No, that's nice.

227
00:10:01,350 --> 00:10:03,978
I like people that bring
their own Uber snacks...

228
00:10:04,061 --> 00:10:06,063
-[Barry laughs]
-...in their back pocket.

229
00:10:08,274 --> 00:10:09,483
So you're losing weight,

230
00:10:09,775 --> 00:10:11,777
-you got a fantastic girlfriend.
-I told her--

231
00:10:11,861 --> 00:10:14,030
-You got a pocket full of snacks.
-Yeah.

232
00:10:14,113 --> 00:10:14,989
You're all set.

233
00:10:15,072 --> 00:10:15,948
[jazz plays]

234
00:10:20,786 --> 00:10:22,997
-There it is, Big Lots.
-I knew there was one around here.

235
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:24,790
I've always wanted to go to a Big Lots.

236
00:10:29,211 --> 00:10:30,796
You know what I like about Big Lots?

237
00:10:31,547 --> 00:10:33,466
-[Barry] What?
-The exclamation point.

238
00:10:33,716 --> 00:10:36,385
-That's what you like about 'em?
-I like the enthusiasm.

239
00:10:36,469 --> 00:10:38,262
I like that they're excited about it.

240
00:10:38,346 --> 00:10:40,222
-Yeah.
-I've never been in one of these stores.

241
00:10:40,306 --> 00:10:41,641
I have. You like big?

242
00:10:41,724 --> 00:10:42,725
-Yeah.
-You like lots?

243
00:10:42,808 --> 00:10:43,642
-Yeah.
-You got it.

244
00:10:44,852 --> 00:10:46,354
-[man] Hi.
-[Jerry] How are you?

245
00:10:46,896 --> 00:10:49,106
[Jerry] I could wander around
a store like this for hours.

246
00:10:49,190 --> 00:10:50,191
[Barry] Why?

247
00:10:50,566 --> 00:10:53,110
I just like things in packages

248
00:10:53,194 --> 00:10:55,029
and with words on them.

249
00:10:55,571 --> 00:10:56,572
That's everything.

250
00:10:56,656 --> 00:10:57,782
[Jerry laughs]

251
00:10:58,699 --> 00:10:59,950
-Really.
-No, it's not.

252
00:11:00,034 --> 00:11:02,370
-Yosemite is not like that.
-This could be used as a weapon.

253
00:11:02,453 --> 00:11:03,287
[Jerry laughs]

254
00:11:03,371 --> 00:11:06,207
You could literally fend off someone
with a Classic Cookie Collection.

255
00:11:06,791 --> 00:11:07,625
Really.

256
00:11:08,084 --> 00:11:11,504
You start jabbing at somebody with that,
you got a weapon, my friend. Yeah.

257
00:11:11,587 --> 00:11:13,339
"Back off,
I got a Classic Cookie Collection."

258
00:11:13,422 --> 00:11:15,091
[jazz plays]

259
00:11:17,426 --> 00:11:20,429
Let me get this straight, you like things
that have writing on them.

260
00:11:20,596 --> 00:11:26,018
Yes. I like boxes and containers
with writing on it.

261
00:11:26,519 --> 00:11:28,938
'Cause the writing
tells you what's in there.

262
00:11:30,106 --> 00:11:31,774
Nobody's more observant than you.

263
00:11:32,983 --> 00:11:35,152
Oh, look at these cereals. Wow!

264
00:11:35,528 --> 00:11:38,739
-This is really where you shine.
-[Jerry] Oh, baby. Bring it on.

265
00:11:38,823 --> 00:11:41,033
Let me tell you what it's all about
right now.

266
00:11:41,117 --> 00:11:42,868
What's your favorite cereal, all-time?

267
00:11:42,952 --> 00:11:45,579
I would probably have to say
Kellogg's Bran Flakes.

268
00:11:45,663 --> 00:11:46,831
-Bran flakes?
-I like them.

269
00:11:46,914 --> 00:11:48,541
No wonder you had a depressed childhood.

270
00:11:50,876 --> 00:11:52,920
[Jerry] I like the acknowledgment...

271
00:11:54,004 --> 00:11:57,258
that you're gonna need it.
Why are you just getting one?

272
00:11:57,341 --> 00:11:58,843
Why don't I just get a lot?

273
00:11:58,926 --> 00:12:00,928
Why you're not in timeshare business,
I don't know.

274
00:12:01,011 --> 00:12:02,638
-This is fun, isn't it?
-Yeah.

275
00:12:02,722 --> 00:12:04,098
[jazz plays]

276
00:12:04,890 --> 00:12:06,809
You were working at The Comedy Store,

277
00:12:06,892 --> 00:12:08,519
and you were getting by

278
00:12:08,602 --> 00:12:10,521
selling jokes to other comedians.

279
00:12:10,688 --> 00:12:12,940
I was selling jokes to Jay Leno,
and Letterman,

280
00:12:13,023 --> 00:12:14,942
and Garry Shandling, and Bill Maher,

281
00:12:15,234 --> 00:12:17,486
and I was getting $75 a joke back then.

282
00:12:17,570 --> 00:12:18,988
Seventy-five dollars a joke?

283
00:12:19,071 --> 00:12:20,990
So, my father says to me,
"How you making money?"

284
00:12:21,073 --> 00:12:22,324
And I explained that to him.

285
00:12:22,408 --> 00:12:24,952
He would say, "Why would they pay you
that much money for a joke?"

286
00:12:25,035 --> 00:12:27,079
And I'd go, "Because the people
that need the jokes

287
00:12:27,163 --> 00:12:28,747
-really need them."
-[Jerry laughs]

288
00:12:28,831 --> 00:12:30,124
The guys that need 'em...

289
00:12:30,207 --> 00:12:32,001
-really need 'em.
-Really need them.

290
00:12:34,128 --> 00:12:36,464
-[Barry] I'm here. I'm having fun.
-Me too. I'm having fun.

291
00:12:36,547 --> 00:12:39,049
This is boarding,
particle boarding for a room.

292
00:12:40,176 --> 00:12:42,511
-This is a shelf.
-Yeah. [laughs]

293
00:12:42,595 --> 00:12:45,014
-This is a--
-You could finish a deck with this.

294
00:12:45,097 --> 00:12:48,809
How much bigger candy do you want,
for God's sake? This is a sampler?

295
00:12:48,893 --> 00:12:51,395
-That's not even a whole box?
-No, it's just a sample.

296
00:12:51,479 --> 00:12:52,313
Yeah.

297
00:12:52,396 --> 00:12:54,106
If you like it,
we'll give you the big one.

298
00:12:54,190 --> 00:12:55,649
[laughs] Yeah.

299
00:12:56,776 --> 00:12:57,735
By the way,

300
00:12:57,818 --> 00:13:01,280
do you recall, it wasn't too long ago
that Twinkie went out of business,

301
00:13:01,363 --> 00:13:03,240
and then somebody swooped in
and bought the name?

302
00:13:03,324 --> 00:13:04,366
-I think so.
-Remember that?

303
00:13:04,450 --> 00:13:06,076
-Yep.
-So, it's exciting that they're back.

304
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:07,328
-You wanna get a Twinkie?
-Yes!

305
00:13:08,579 --> 00:13:10,456
[Jerry] Look at this, a chocolate Twinkie.

306
00:13:10,539 --> 00:13:12,333
-I never even heard of this.
-You haven't?

307
00:13:12,416 --> 00:13:14,752
-I never heard of this.
-Come to my house later.

308
00:13:15,294 --> 00:13:16,545
That looks good, fudge covered.

309
00:13:16,629 --> 00:13:18,589
-Think we could lose weight eating this?
-Yeah.

310
00:13:18,672 --> 00:13:20,674
[woman] Do you have
a rewards card with us?

311
00:13:20,758 --> 00:13:21,884
[Barry] A rewards card?

312
00:13:21,967 --> 00:13:23,803
This is the reward right here.

313
00:13:24,887 --> 00:13:27,515
This is gonna be fun.
This is my idea of a fun day.

314
00:13:27,598 --> 00:13:29,308
[jazz plays]

315
00:13:39,735 --> 00:13:41,237
[Jerry eating] This is really good.

316
00:13:45,324 --> 00:13:48,953
Drake's came out with a Yodel,
and it came in a box, and each Drake...

317
00:13:49,537 --> 00:13:50,496
Am I boring you?

318
00:13:50,579 --> 00:13:51,622
[both laugh]

319
00:13:52,248 --> 00:13:53,666
[upbeat music plays]

320
00:13:53,749 --> 00:13:56,710
-[woman] Jerry, I love the show!
-[Jerry] Thank you!

321
00:13:57,086 --> 00:13:58,504
[Jerry] You too, sweetie.

322
00:14:02,508 --> 00:14:03,592
Is that, um...

323
00:14:04,468 --> 00:14:05,886
-inappropriate?
-What?

324
00:14:05,970 --> 00:14:08,597
-I called her "sweetie." Is that...
-That's a write-up.

325
00:14:08,681 --> 00:14:09,515
[laughs]

326
00:14:11,809 --> 00:14:14,270
Highlight of my day so far
is the Twinkies.

327
00:14:14,728 --> 00:14:15,646
No offense.

328
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:17,982
[upbeat music plays]

329
00:14:23,737 --> 00:14:25,948
-[woman] Hi, welcome to BevMo!
-Thank you.

330
00:14:26,448 --> 00:14:27,283
I love it.

331
00:14:27,366 --> 00:14:29,159
I hope I can find something to drink.

332
00:14:29,243 --> 00:14:30,077
[jazz plays]

333
00:14:32,746 --> 00:14:34,999
[Jerry whispering] It's a lot of booze.
It's all booze.

334
00:14:35,082 --> 00:14:37,167
I thought it was
all different kinds of beverages.

335
00:14:37,251 --> 00:14:38,377
-It's just booze.
-Booze.

336
00:14:38,460 --> 00:14:41,797
Does everybody that comes in this store
have a problem?

337
00:14:41,881 --> 00:14:44,425
Everyone that goes into any store
has a problem, Jerry.

338
00:14:44,717 --> 00:14:46,677
Very few people are free of any problems.

339
00:14:49,179 --> 00:14:51,599
-I really like cute, little things.
-Why don't you buy that?

340
00:14:51,682 --> 00:14:53,475
I don't like the flavor of this stuff.

341
00:14:53,559 --> 00:14:55,895
I'd rather get some more Twinkie products.

342
00:14:56,729 --> 00:14:58,230
[upbeat jazz plays]

343
00:14:59,899 --> 00:15:02,151
Now, tomorrow,
I'm going to get my ear fixed.

344
00:15:02,484 --> 00:15:03,986
-What's that?
-I'm getting my ear fix--

345
00:15:04,069 --> 00:15:05,446
-That was a joke.
-Oh.

346
00:15:06,238 --> 00:15:07,698
I hope the guy can do it.

347
00:15:07,781 --> 00:15:10,743
Well, out of the two people in the room,
he would have the best shot.

348
00:15:12,286 --> 00:15:14,830
My brother and I hang out.
We sit and we watch TV. We have fun.

349
00:15:14,914 --> 00:15:17,499
Well, he lives in your house.
What choice do you have?

350
00:15:18,834 --> 00:15:21,170
-Al Jaffee's snappy answers...
-[Jerry laughs]

351
00:15:21,253 --> 00:15:22,630
...to roommate situations.

352
00:15:22,713 --> 00:15:25,007
"We pass each other in the hall."
"Well, he lives there."

353
00:15:25,090 --> 00:15:27,468
"I see him around a lot."
"Yeah, you're roommates."

354
00:15:27,551 --> 00:15:29,762
-"He was in the garage, getting his car."
-Yeah.

355
00:15:30,262 --> 00:15:31,513
[Jerry laughs]

356
00:15:32,598 --> 00:15:34,808
Such a nice ride in this car today.

357
00:15:35,100 --> 00:15:37,227
Enjoyable being outside in the sun,

358
00:15:38,020 --> 00:15:40,356
in an open car that runs nice,

359
00:15:40,689 --> 00:15:42,441
just like it was six years ago.

360
00:15:43,567 --> 00:15:45,027
I don't know what I'd do without you.

361
00:15:45,110 --> 00:15:46,654
I don't know what I'd do without you.

362
00:16:03,462 --> 00:16:05,798
-What are you gonna do now?
-Um, nothing.

363
00:16:05,881 --> 00:16:06,840
-Thank you again.
-Okay.

364
00:16:06,924 --> 00:16:08,634
-Appreciate it. Thanks.
-Thank you.

365
00:16:08,717 --> 00:16:10,886
[sentimental instrumental music plays]

366
00:16:25,401 --> 00:16:26,819
[door lock opens]

367
00:16:33,325 --> 00:16:35,202
Jerry, it's... it's over.

368
00:16:35,285 --> 00:16:36,662
-All right.
-Thank you.

369
00:16:37,579 --> 00:16:38,414
Really.

370
00:16:38,497 --> 00:16:40,124
All right, all right, I'm going.

371
00:16:41,083 --> 00:16:42,209
Aye. [sighs]

